# Engineering Responsibilities

This document owns:

* Authorization architecture
* Permission model
* Access boundaries
* Role responsibilities
* Authorization governance

It does not define authentication, identity verification, permission storage, or implementation-specific access control technologies.

---

# Authorization Philosophy

Authorization determines what authenticated identities are allowed to do.

Its responsibility is to protect business capabilities by ensuring every operation is performed only by identities with the appropriate responsibilities.

Authorization exists to preserve business integrity rather than simply restrict system access.

Permissions should reflect operational responsibilities instead of technical implementation.

---

# Authorization Design Principles

Every authorization decision should prioritize:

* Business responsibility over technical implementation
* Least privilege over unrestricted access
* Explicit permission over implicit inheritance
* Consistency over special-case exceptions
* Long-term maintainability over convenience

Authorization should reinforce business architecture rather than compensate for weak application design.

---

# Permission Architecture

Permissions should represent business capabilities.

Every permission should describe a meaningful business operation rather than a user interface action or technical implementation detail.

Permission models should remain understandable to both engineers and product stakeholders.

Business capabilities should evolve without requiring fundamental changes to the authorization architecture.

---

# Role Responsibilities

Roles exist to group related operational responsibilities.

A role represents the expected responsibilities of a user within an organization.

Roles should simplify authorization while remaining independent of individual users.

Responsibilities should remain stable even as individual permissions evolve.

---

# Access Boundaries

Authorization should preserve organizational and architectural boundaries.

Permissions should never allow identities to operate outside the responsibilities intentionally granted by the platform.

Every protected business capability should verify authorization before execution.

Boundary enforcement should remain consistent across every interaction channel.

---

# Business Integrity

Authorization protects business operations.

Access decisions should preserve operational correctness regardless of client application, integration method, or user interface.

Business rules should never depend on trusted clients or hidden user interface controls.

Authorization should consistently enforce business responsibilities throughout the platform.

---

# Authorization Consistency

Equivalent business capabilities should enforce authorization consistently.

Consistency should be maintained across:

* User interfaces
* APIs
* Background processing
* Realtime communication
* External integrations

Authorization behavior should remain predictable regardless of how business capabilities are accessed.

---

# Authorization Evolution

Authorization architecture should evolve alongside business capabilities.

New permissions should strengthen existing responsibility models rather than introduce fragmented access strategies.

Evolution should improve clarity while preserving consistency throughout the platform.

Authorization should become more expressive without becoming more complicated.

---

# Authorization Governance

Authorization decisions should be intentional.

Before introducing a new permission or role, evaluate:

* Does this represent a meaningful business capability?
* Can an existing permission satisfy the requirement?
* Does this preserve least privilege?
* Will future engineers understand the responsibility model?
* Does it strengthen long-term maintainability?

Authorization architecture should become more coherent over time rather than increasingly fragmented.

---

# Permissions Quality Gates

Authorization architecture remains healthy when:

* Permissions represent business capabilities.
* Roles reflect operational responsibilities.
* Least privilege guides access decisions.
* Access boundaries remain clearly defined.
* Business integrity is consistently protected.
* Authorization evolves alongside business capabilities.
* Responsibility models remain understandable.
* Authorization decisions remain aligned with the Engineering Constitution.

This document defines the authorization engineering standards for the hubIO platform.

Implementation guidance is defined by the Engineering Constitution and the relevant Engineering Standards.
